Leonix
======

Leonix is a kernel for the Arduino Leonardo (ATmega32U4), written in C
and AVR assembly and built with avr-gcc.  It does not use the Arduino
core or any of its libraries.

The board is meant to run on its own, powered from the barrel jack or
VIN.  When a computer is attached, the USB port is a CDC-ACM serial
port that carries the kernel log, and flashing needs no press of the
reset button.

A Chinese version of this file is in README.zh_CN.


Status
------

A 1 ms Timer1 tick drives a preemptive round-robin scheduler.  Tasks
sleep until a deadline instead of spinning, and an idle task puts the
CPU into Idle mode when none can run.  main() starts three tasks: two
blink the L and TX LEDs, the third drives an HD44780 LCD through a
PCF8574 I2C backpack, showing the uptime and how idle the CPU is, and
reinitialises it after the backpack is unplugged and plugged back.
A fourth task runs the USB device.

printk() keeps the last 16 messages.  Opening the serial port replays
them and then follows new ones:

	cat /dev/cu.usbmodemXXXX

All of this runs on the board.  With the LCD refreshed once a second
the display read 99% idle before the USB task was added.

	make
	make flash PORT=/dev/cu.usbmodemXXXX

With Leonix running, make flash restarts the board into the bootloader
itself.  An older image needs the reset button pressed first.


Hardware
--------

  - Arduino Leonardo (A000057): ATmega32U4 at 16 MHz, 32 KiB flash of
    which 28 KiB is left to the application by the Caterina
    bootloader, 2.5 KiB SRAM, 1 KiB EEPROM.
  - Console: an HD44780 16x2 character LCD behind a PCF8574 I2C
    backpack, SDA on D2 and SCL on D3.


Toolchain
---------

  - avr-gcc (developed with 9.5.0) and binutils for AVR
  - avrdude, talking AVR109 to the Caterina bootloader

Development is on macOS.  The tree also builds with the toolchains
packaged by Debian, Ubuntu, Fedora and Arch Linux, avr-gcc 5.4.0 to
16.2.0; only the macOS image has been run on the board.  Windows is
untested.  Documentation/building.rst has the install commands and
the serial port name for each system.

make flash enters the Caterina bootloader by opening the port at 1200
baud and closing it, as the Arduino core does.
Documentation/notes/usb.rst has the details.


Documentation
-------------

Documentation/building.rst
	Installing the toolchain, building the image and flashing it
	through the Caterina bootloader.  "make htmldocs" renders all of
	Documentation/ to HTML with Sphinx.

Documentation/booting.rst
	The machine state the Caterina bootloader leaves behind when it
	jumps to the application, and what the start code has to undo.

Documentation/notes/index.rst
	Design notes on the AVR architecture itself, the start code, the
	I/O pins, the system tick, the scheduler, the I2C LCD, the kernel
	log and the USB serial port, written
	for readers new to kernel work.  Read them in
	the order listed there.

Documentation/references/index.rst
	Every datasheet and external source the project relies on, with
	document number and revision.

Only the Leonardo schematic is shipped in the tree, because it is the
only reference released under a licence that allows redistribution
(CC BY-SA 4.0).  The other datasheets have to be fetched separately:

	cd Documentation/references
	curl -L -o ATmega16U4-32U4-datasheet-7766J.pdf \
	  https://ww1.microchip.com/downloads/en/DeviceDoc/Atmel-7766-8-bit-AVR-ATmega16U4-32U4_Datasheet.pdf
	curl -L -o AVR-InstructionSet-Manual-DS40002198.pdf \
	  https://ww1.microchip.com/downloads/aemDocuments/documents/MCU08/ProductDocuments/ReferenceManuals/AVR-InstructionSet-Manual-DS40002198.pdf
	curl -L -o HD44780U-Hitachi.pdf \
	  https://cdn.sparkfun.com/assets/9/5/f/7/b/HD44780.pdf
	curl -L -o PCF8574-TI.pdf \
	  https://www.ti.com/lit/ds/symlink/pcf8574.pdf
	curl -L -o UM10204-I2C-NXP.pdf \
	  https://www.nxp.com/docs/en/user-guide/UM10204.pdf
	curl -L -o AVR-C-Compiler-CoDesign-Myklebust.pdf \
	  https://ic.unicamp.br/~celio/mc404-2004/Atmel_AVR/C_compiler.pdf

The USB specifications come as zip archives.
Documentation/references/index.rst names the file to take from each:

	curl -L -O https://www.usb.org/sites/default/files/usb_20_20240604.zip
	curl -L -O https://www.usb.org/sites/default/files/CDC1.2_WMC1.1_012011.zip

These files are not covered by .gitignore.  Add their names to
.git/info/exclude so they are not committed by accident.


